" (https://rnajc.ucsf.edu/sites/rnajc.ucsf.edu/files/nbt.3973_0.pdf)\nciteturn46view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn36view3\",\"lineno\":681}); Total lines: 942\nL647@P4: AbBs. For the third condition, 0.2 mg/ml of dextran sulfate (DS) in PBS with 1%\nL648@P4: BSA was added to cells for 10 min on ice to reduce non-specific binding of the\nL649@P4: negatively charged DNA barcodes to the cellular membrane (Supplementary\nL650@P4: Fig. 5). The AbB mix (n = 45) consisted of 1 μl per antibody (total of 45 μL)\nL651@P4: and the final reaction volume was 200 μL (0.1 mg/ml DS in PBS with 1% BSA).\nL652@P4: Cells were labeled with the AbB mix for 15 min on ice. After staining, 12 ml of\nL653@P4: the wash buffer (PBS with 1% BSA or 0.1 mg/ml DS in PBS) was added to the cells.\nL654@P4: The samples were spun down at 300g for 4 min at 4 °C, and the supernatant was\nL655@P4: removed with a new 10-ml serological pipette for each sample to prevent cross\u0002contamination. The supernatant was carefully removed, leaving volume of ~50\nL656@P4: μl remaining to avoid disturbing the cell pellet. The washing step was repeated\nL657@P4: three times, and a final wash was conducted in PBS with 1% BSA for all samples.\nL658@P4: After the last wash, PBMCs blocked with dextran sulfate before labeling with\nL659@P4: AbBs were split into four conditions: 1) no magnetic enrichment, 2) CD3+ mag\u0002netic enrichment (Miltenyi, 130-050-101), 3) CD11b+ magnetic enrichment\nL660@P4: (Miltenyi, 130-049-601), and 4) CD19+ magnetic enrichment (Miltenyi, 130-\nL661@P4: 050-301). Miltenyi microbeads were used to positively select for CD3+ T cells,\nL662@P4: CD11b+ myeloid cells, or CD19+ B cells using MS columns (Miltenyi, 130-042-\nL663@P4: 201) following manufacturer’s instructions.\nL664@P4: Flow cytometry PBMC immunophenotyping. Frozen PBMCs (AllCells,\nL665@P4: PB003F, LotA5051 (same donor as for REAP-seq)) were quick-thawed in a 37\nL666@P4: °C water bath, washed with 30 mL pre-warmed RPMI Complete Medium (RPMI\nL667@P4: 1640 + 10% FBS + Pen/Strep + l-glutamine), and washed again with 25 mL\nL668@P4: Wash Buffer (1× DPBS + 2% FBS + 1 mM EDTA). Viability was assessed using\nL669@P4: the Vi-Cell Cell Counter (Beckman Coulter) using default settings. Cells were\nL670@P4: washed a second time in 25 mL Wash Buffer, and the cell pellet was resuspended\nL671@P4: in a viability stain solution (5.4 mL 1× DPBS + 13.5 μL Fixable Viability Dye\nL672@P4: eFluor506 (eBioscience)). After 30 min incubation on ice, the cells were washed\nL673@P4: in 25 mL wash buffer. One million viable cells were aliquoted into microfuge\nL674@P4: tubes for cell surface staining. Fc-receptors were blocked in a solution contain\u0002ing 45 μL wash buffer, 5 μL Human TruStain FcX (BioLegend), and 2% normal\nL675@P4: mouse serum (Jackson ImmunoResearch). A cocktail of cell-surface-staining\nL676@P4: antibodies (Supplementary Table 5) was added directly to cells in blocking buf\u0002fer. Cells were stained for 30 min on ice and then washed with wash buffer. All\nL677@P4: samples were then fixed with 100 μL freshly diluted 1.6% formaldehyde (Electron\nL678@P4: Microscopy Sciences) in DPBS per tube for 15 min at room temperature. The\nL679@P4: cells were washed with 1 mL wash buffer, resuspended in 500 μL wash buffer,\nL680@P4: and acquired on a LSR II flow cytometer (BD Biosciences). Cytobank (v5.6.1)\nL681@P4: was used for data analysis21. All flow cytometry data shown were initially gated\nL682@P4: on live cells (Supplementary Fig. 24a).\nL683@P4: aCD27 ex vivo CD8+ T-cell activation assay. Human buffy coats from three\nL684@P4: healthy volunteers were obtained from the Stanford Blood Center. All blood\nL685@P4: samples were collected from voluntary donors after obtaining informed consent,\nL686@P4: in accordance with institutional review board protocols and in compliance with\nL687@P4: state and federal regulations. The method used for purification of naive CD8+\nL688@P4: T cells (which make up typically ~0.4–2.6% leukocytes) involved two negative\u0002selection enrichment steps, where the first enriched for CD8+ cells and the second\nL689@P4: enriched for naive CD8+ T cells, using a cocktail of antibodies targeting lineage\u0002associated (Lin) antigens. In the first enrichment step, human CD8+ T cells were\nL690@P4: obtained from buffy coats using RosetteSep Human CD8+ T Cell Enrichment\nL691@P4: Cocktail (StemCell Technologies) via negative selection according to manufac\u0002turer’s instructions. Briefly, cells were stained with the RosetteSep cocktail of\nL692@P4: antibodies and centrifuged over a buoyant density medium Ficoll-Paque Plus\nL693@P4: (GE, Healthcare). The enriched CD8+ T cells were removed from the plasma\nL694@P4: and density medium interface and lysed with ACK lysis buffer (Thermo Fisher).\nL695@P4: In the second enrichment step, naive CD8+ T cells were then negatively selected\nL696@P4: for using a Human Naive CD8+ T Cell Enrichment Set (BD Biosciences) contain\u0002ing biotinylated monoclonal antibodies (CD4, CD11b, CD19, CD16, CD41a,\nL697@P4: CD45RO, gd-TCR, Glycophorin A) to remove CD4+ T cells, monocytes, B cells,\nL698@P4: NK cells, granulocytes, platelets, memory T cells, g/d T cells, and erythroid cells.\nL699@P4: Cells were frozen in liquid nitrogen for future use. It has been shown that rare\nL700@P4: cell types such as hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) which do not express Lin\nL701@P4: antigens can be enriched by 50- to 200-fold from peripheral blood using cock\u0002tails of antibodies to remove Lin+ cells22,23. Since our purification method used\nL702@P4: a double negative selection, it is plausible that a rare population of cells such as\nL703@P4: common myeloid progenitors could be enriched (>200-fold) and detected at\nL704@P4: ~2–5% of total cells.\nL705@P4: Humanized anti-human CD27 antibody (MSD) was bound to Dynabeads\nL706@P4: (M-450 Epoxy, Life Technologies) by mixing overnight with prewashed\nL707@P4: Dynabeads at room temperature. BSA was added to block remaining free bind\u0002ing sites on the beads. Beads were washed and resuspended to ~1.5 × 105 beads\nL708@P4: per μL in 0.1 M sodium phosphate buffer.\nL709@P4: For the activation assay to determine an aCD27-specific gene and protein\nL710@P4: signature, CD8+ naive T cells were thawed and resuspended to 7.5 × 105 cells/\nL711@P4: ml in DMEM-F12 (Gibco), 5% heat-inactivated human serum (Sigma), 50 μM\nL712@P4: 2-mercaptoethanol (Sigma), 100 U/ml penicillin, and 100 μg/ml streptomycin\nL713@P4: (Lonza). 1.5 × 105 cells were cultured in the presence of a sub-optimal dose of\nL714@P4: aCD3 (0.025–0.15 mg/ml clone OKT3, Biolegend) and aCD28 (1 mg/ml clone\nL715@P4: 15E8, Millipore) with or without 1 μL of bead-bound anti-CD27 in a flat-bottom\nL716@P4: 96-well plate for 3 d in a 37 °C, 5% CO2 incubator. Following stimulation, an\nL717@P4: aliquot of cells was washed and frozen in 90% FBS (Hyclone) and 10% DMSO\nL718@P4: (Sigma) for downstream REAP-seq processing. Another aliquot of cells was\nL719@P4: stained with a fixable viability dye (eFluor506, eBioscience) for 30 min at 4 °C.\nL720@P4: Excess dye was removed by washing and the cells were blocked with TruStain FcX\nL721@P4: (Biolegend). The cells were then incubated and stained with a cocktail of antibod\u0002ies (Supplementary Table 5) and analyzed by flow cytometry (BD Biosciences\nL722@P4: LSRFortessa) for comparison to REAP-seq proteomic measurements. All flow\nL723@P4: cytometry data shown were initially gated on live cells (Supplementary Fig. 24b).\nL724@P4: aCD27-treated and untreated cells from donors 1, 2, and 3 were thawed at\nL725@P4: 37 °C for 3 min and resuspended by adding 10 ml of PBS with 1% BSA slowly\nL726@P4-5: © 2017 Nature America, Inc., part of Springer Nature. All rights reserved.NATURE BIOTECHNOLOGY doi:10.1038/nbt.3973\nL727@P5: in a dropwise manner. The cells were blocked with 0.2 mg/ml of dextran sulfate\nL728@P5: in PBS with 1% BSA for 10 min on ice to reduce non-specific binding of the\nL729@P5: negatively charged oligonucleotides to the cellular membrane. The AbB mix\nL730@P5: (Supplementary Table 1) consisted of 1 μl of each antibody (80 antibodies, total\nL731@P5: of 80 μl) and the labeling was performed as previously described above for the\nL732@P5: REAP-seq PBMC experiment.\nL733@P5: A Sony SH800 sorter (Sony Biotechnology) was used to enrich for live cells\nL734@P5: stained with the LIVE/DEAD Viability/Cytotoxicity kit (Thermo Fisher). Cells\nL735@P5: were resuspended in 300 μl of calcein AM (0.2 μM) and ethidium homodimer-1\nL736@P5: (0.2 μM) in PBS with 1% BSA to discriminate live from dead cells where viability\nL737@P5: ranged from ~50–75%. REAP-seq was conducted on cells treated with aCD27\nL738@P5: (donor 1, 4,246; donor 2, 4,044; donor 3, 3,550 cells) and without aCD27 (donor\nL739@P5: 1, 950; donor 2, 622; donor 3, 406). Fewer aCD27 untreated cells were analyzed\nL740@P5: due to lower (~2–3×) initial starting cell numbers and also loss of a sample due\nL741@P5: to an emulsion break (donor 2 and 3, ~2× decrease).\nL742@P5: REAP-seq assay. Cell number and viability were measured using a Countess II\nL743@P5: Automated Cell Counter (ThermoFisher). Cells were loaded onto the Chromium\nL744@P5: Controller (10x Genomics) targeting 10,000 cells per lane; for samples with\nL745@P5: <10,000 cells, the entire sample was loaded. The 10x Genomics v1 single cell\nL746@P5: 3ʹ RNA-seq reagent kit protocol (10x Genomics) was used to process samples\nL747@P5: according to manufacturer’s protocol. Following the reverse transcription and\nL748@P5: the silane bead cleanup step there is a 0.6× SPRI size-selection step (Beckman\nL749@P5: Coulter, B23318) where ~80 μL of supernatant (50 μl sample + 30 μl SPRI) is\nL750@P5: discarded. This supernatant contains the shorter (~155 bp) cell barcoded AbBs\nL751@P5: (CB-AbBs) and is retained for the proteomic part of this assay (Supplementary\nL752@P5: Fig. 3).\nL753@P5: SPRI beads (45 μl) were added to the supernatant containing the CB-AbBs\nL754@P5: (~80 μl) and incubated at room temperature for 5 min. The SPRI beads were\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn46view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn21view1\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLarge-scale plasma proteomics comparisons through genetics and disease associations | Nature (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x)\nciteturn46view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn36view2\",\"lineno\":130}); Redirected to URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x?error=cookies_not_supported&code=b95dab81-05d0-469b-9576-a88c2a0ac7c5; Total lines: 991\nL120: In the Icelandic set of 1,514 individuals with data from both platforms, the median Spearman correlation between plasma levels of 1,848 proteins measured with matching Olink and SomaScan assays was 0.33 (Fig. cite97†1 , Supplementary Tables cite99†5 and cite98†6 , Supplementary Note cite99†5 and Supplementary Fig. cite98†2 ), consistent with previous reports based on up to around 900 proteins^{cite89†7 ,cite93†10 ,cite94†11 }.\nL121: The distribution of the correlation coefficients has two modes, one just above 0 and the other just below 0.6. Omitting the SMP normalization step on SomaScan, we observed a higher median correlation of 0.39 between platforms. The Olink Explore 3072 consists of two assay sets: an earlier version of the platform labelled 1536, and an additional follow-up set of assays labelled Expansion.\nL122: We note a substantially higher median correlation for assays in the 1536 set than in the Expansion set (0.36 and 0.27, respectively). For comparison, the median pairwise correlation between assays from the two platforms for all possible assay pairs was −0.01, and the median within-platform correlation between assays was 0.08 for the Olink platform and −0.01 for the SomaScan platform (0.42 if omitting SMP normalization).\nL123: If either assay of a matching pair had a low variation in levels, the correlation between the levels of proteins measured with the matching assays tended to be low (Supplementary Notes cite99†4 –cite99†6 and Supplementary Fig. cite99†3 ).\nL124: The overall variance of the levels of a protein was more concordant between matching assays on the two platforms where cis pQTLs were detected on both platforms (Spearman correlation 0.57 for pQTLs on both platforms, otherwise below 0.28) but did not show a clear trend for individual platforms depending on the presence or absence of cis pQTLs on that platform (Extended Data Fig. cite103†2 ).\nL125: ## Dilution and subcellular location\nL126: Both Olink and SomaScan use dilution groups for their assays based on the protein abundance in plasma, with the most abundant proteins belonging to the highest dilution groups (Supplementary Table cite99†7 ). For both platforms, the correlation between protein levels was lowest in the lowest dilution group (Supplementary Table cite99†8 ).\nL127: Correspondingly, for the matching assays, the median CV was higher in the lowest dilution group than in the other dilution groups, although on the SomaScan platform, the CV was again higher in the highest dilution group than the intermediate group (Supplementary Table cite99†9 ). A substantial number of Olink assays have levels below the limit of detection (LOD) for a large fraction of individuals, especially for proteins in the undiluted group—that is, with expected lowest abundance in plasma.\nL128: The fraction of values below LOD correlated positively with CV (Spearman correlation 0.69, P < 10^{–300}; Supplementary Fig. cite99†4 ).\nL129: According to the Human Protein Atlas, 63% of the 19,187 human proteins are intracellular, 28% are membrane proteins and the remaining 9% are secreted^{cite104†14 }. SomaScan v4 and Olink Explore 3072 were similarly depleted of intracellular proteins (49% and 48%, respectively) and enriched with secreted proteins (21% and 24%, respectively) (P < 5.7 × 10^{−158}; Supplementary Table cite99†10 ).\nL130: The protein abundance as reflected by the dilution groups is lower on both platforms for assays targeting intracellular proteins.\nL131: The abundance of proteins as reflected by the dilution group is associated with the precision of measurements (Supplementary Tables cite99†9 and cite99†11 and Supplementary Fig. cite99†5 ). Previous reports have described the effect of decreased matrix complexity on measurement, which along with the abundance may affect the precision^{cite105†15 }.\nL132: We noted a wide range of median correlation between levels when stratifying on tissue based on enriched expression (r between 0.05 and 0.64). The tissue with the lowest median correlation was the pituitary gland, followed by testis, fallopian tube, retina, skin and brain (all with r ≤ 0.15). The tissue with the highest median correlation of levels was gallbladder, followed by smooth muscle, cervix, endometrium, pancreas and salivary gland (all with r > 0.45; Supplementary Table cite98†12 ).\nL133: ## Protein–phenotype associations\nL134: We accounted for multiple testing of protein–phenotype associations using Bonferroni adjustment for the number of assays on each platform (P < 1.0 × 10^{−5} for 4,907 assays on SomaScan and P < 1.7 × 10^{−5} for 2,941 assays on Olink). Plasma protein levels have previously been shown to correlate with sample age^{cite106†16 }.\nL135: Sample age—that is, time from blood draw to measurement—correlated significantly with levels of a considerable number of proteins on both platforms, although differences in the distribution of sample age between cohorts make direct comparison difficult (Supplementary Tables cite99†13 –cite98†15 ). The effects of sample age were generally small for both platforms and the correlation between effects was low (Spearman r = 0.08).\nL136: The levels of 77% of SomaScan proteins were associated with participant age at sample collection, 64% were associated with sex and 69% were associated with body mass index (BMI).\nL137: In UKB-BI, the levels of 60%, 68% and 78% of Olink proteins were associated with participant age at sample collection, sex and BMI, respectively, and the effects correlated well between ancestries in UKB (pairwise Spearman correlation greater than 0.70, 0.71 and 0.87 for participant age, sex and BMI, respectively; Extended Data Fig. cite107†3 and Supplementary Tables cite98†14 and cite98†15 ).\nL138: The Spearman correlation of participant age and sex effects between the 2,021 pairs of matching assays for proteins targeted by both Olink and SomaScan was 0.52 for participant age, 0.56 for sex and 0.43 for BMI.\nL139: We tested for associations between protein levels and 389 binary phenotypes and 208 quantitative traits in UKB, and 275 binary phenotypes and 110 quantitative traits in Iceland. In UKB, we found 303,261, 13,047 and 10,850 associations among individuals with British or Irish, South Asian and African ancestries, respectively (Supplementary Table cite98†16 ). In Iceland, we found 218,503 associations (Supplementary Table cite98†17 ).\nL140: As examples, we compared the associations between levels of proteins measured using the two platforms with heart failure, Alzheimer’s disease and inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) (comprising Crohn’s disease and ulcerative colitis).\nL141: For heart failure (n = 1,369 cases in Iceland and n = 676 cases in UKB), natriuretic peptide B (BNP) (encoded by NPPB) had the most significant association on both platforms (UKB-BI with Olink: odds ratio (OR) = 2.25 per s.d., P = 1.6 × 10^{−177}; Iceland with SomaScan: OR = 1.90 per s.d., p = 2.0 × 10^{−163}), consistent with the established correlation of circulating BNP levels with heart failure^{cite108†17 }, whereas for some proteins the results were discordant (Supplementary Note cite99†7 ).\nL142: In the case of Alzheimer’s disease (n = 389 cases in Iceland and n = 224 cases in UKB-BI), neurofilament light (NFL) (encoded by NEFL) was targeted on both platforms and strongly associated with disease on both (rank 2 of all assays on Olink and rank 3 of all assays on SomaScan based on effect and significance), but with opposite direction (UKB-BI Olink: OR = 1.64 per s.d., P = 1.3 × 10^{−12}; SomaScan OR = 0.53 per s.d., P = 9.7 × 10^{−52}).\nL143: The Spearman correlation between levels of NFL on the two platforms was low (r = 0.06) and cis pQTLs for the protein were not detected on either platform. Many proteoforms can be derived from a single gene, for example through differential splicing of transcripts, proteolytic cleavage and post-translational modification^{cite109†18 }. NFL forms polymers^{cite110†19 }, and we do not have information about which proteoforms are measured on the two platforms.\nL144: Consistent with the results for NFL on Olink, high NFL levels in cerebrospinal fluid and blood have been reported to associate with advanced Alzheimer’s disease^{cite111†20 }. Plasma levels of NFL measured using the Olink platform and an alternative affinity-based assay (Simoa) have been reported to be strongly correlated^{cite112†21 ,cite113†22 } (r > 0.90 for both studies), whereas levels of NFL measured using the SomaScan platform did not correlate with Simoa measurements (n = 231 Icelanders, Spearman r = 0.00).\nL145: Of note, NFL levels measured with both the Olink and SomaScan platforms correlate strongly with Alzheimer’s disease, but with opposite directions of effect. Although Olink and Simoa appear to be measuring the same proteoform(s), it remains to be understood what protein or proteoform SomaScan is measuring.\nL146: For IBD (n = 618 cases in Iceland and n = 900 cases in UKB-BI), prostaglandin-H2 d-isomerase (encoded by PTGDS) was the most significantly associated protein with the disease on both Olink and SomaScan (Supplementary Table cite98†18 ).\nL147: On both platforms, IBD cases had higher plasma levels of prostaglandin-H2 d-isomerase than controls (UKB-BI with Olink: OR = 1.67 per s.d., P = 4.7 × 10^{−40}; Iceland with SomaScan: OR = 1.36 per s.d., P = 1.9 × 10^{−13}), consistent with the reported role of the PGD2 metabolic pathway in IBD supported by animal models^{cite114†23 ,cite115†24 }. The correlation of levels between platforms was 0.51 and cis pQTLs were observed for PTGDS on both platforms.\nL148: Whereas the level of several proteins as measured on both platforms are significantly associated with IBD (for example, CXCL11 and REG3A), some associations did not replicate between the two groups (for example, interleukin-6 (IL-6), MMP12 and CLPS) (Supplementary Table cite98†18 ).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative studies of 2168 plasma proteins measured by two affinity-based platforms in 4000 Chinese adults | Nature Communications (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-56935-2)\nciteturn46view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn36view1\",\"lineno\":195}); Redirected to URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-56935-2?error=cookies_not_supported&code=7db4f932-bf58-4ae2-950d-c4f1503fdfbd; Total lines: 762\nL186: Furthermore, although previous observational studies suggested a role for Lp-PLA2 (PLA2G7) in the aetiology of cardiovascular diseases, inflammation, and life span^{cite112†23 ,cite113†24 }, the PLA2G7 cis-pQTL was unrelated to risks of major vascular or non-vascular diseases in Chinese adults in CKB^{cite114†25 }, consistent with the findings of clinical trials of LpPLA2-lowering medication^{cite115†26 }.\nL187: It is worth noting that cis-pQTLs for ALDH2 and PLA2G7 are both PAVs, and could be related to potential epitope effects (i.e. genetic variants influencing protein structure and hence reagent binding, instead of protein levels).\nL188: However, it’s unlikely that these two cis-pQTLs are explained by epitope effects alone, as previous studies have reported the effects of these variants on the concentrations of ALDH2 and PLA2G7 proteins measured by different immunoassays^{cite116†27 ,cite117†28 ,cite118†29 }, and our previous studies in CKB also demonstrate a functional impact of the cis-pQTL of ALDH2^{cite109†20 ,cite110†21 ,cite111†22 }.\nL189: Nevertheless, extra caution should be taken when cis-pQTLs are used as genetic instruments in MR studies, as they might only influence assay reagent binding instead of actual plasma levels of the corresponding protein.\nL190: In addition to ancestry-specific cis-pQTLs that colocalised between Olink and SomaScan, we also identified ancestry-specific cis-pQTLs that did not colocalise in CKB. For example, although we identified cis-pQTLs for PCSK9 in both platforms, these did not colocalise. Moreover, neither of them was found in previous studies in European populations, in which the cis-pQTLs for PCSK9 were replicated between Olink and SomaScan^{cite119†9 ,cite77†12 ,cite80†15 ,cite82†16 ,cite83†17 }.\nL191: Such differences in platform concordance might also be explained by differences in genetic architecture across populations: the allele frequency of the CKB Olink cis-pQTL sentinel variant for PCSK9 varies between East Asian and European populations, while the CKB SomaScan cis-pQTL sentinel variant for PCSK9 is very rare in European populations.\nL192: Since the latter variant is a missense variant, it might reflect ancestry-specific epitope effects, although we could not directly test this hypothesis without more information on reagent binding.\nL193: Few previous studies have systematically compared the associations of Olink and SomaScan proteins with traits and risks of disease outcomes in the same sample. In the present study, SomaScan yielded more BMI-associated proteins compared with Olink, while for IHD risk, the converse was true.\nL194: Importantly, the number of BMI-associated proteins (and several other baseline characteristics) was attenuated when the analyses were conducted using SomaScan-ANML data, which normalises protein levels within the ‘normal’ range based on an external reference (healthy individuals) while preserving outliers (which may be affected by disease status)^{cite120†7 ,cite73†8 }.\nL195: This is in contrast with the pQTL analyses, where SomaScan-ANML identified more pQTLs than SomaScan-non-ANML, as revealed by the current study and previous analyses by deCODE^{cite83†17 }. We presented the SomaScan-non-ANML data as the main results, as they were more comparable to Olink data than the SomaScan-ANML data.\nL196: Nevertheless, regardless of the version of the SomaScan data used, we found that proteins with high correlations between the platforms or with colocalising cis-pQTLs tended to yield replicable associations with traits and disease risk across platforms, as shown in Supplementary Fig. cite88†40 and Supplementary Data cite121†7 . This can be illustrated by GHR (associated with BMI in both platforms) and WFDC2 (associated with IHD in both platforms).\nL197: Both proteins showed high concordance in their observational correlations and cis-pQTLs (Supplementary Data cite95†4 ), and their associations with adiposity or with cardiovascular diseases have been previously reported^{cite122†30 ,cite123†31 ,cite124†32 }. This consistency across different platforms will inform researchers when intepreting findings on these proteins, which may be more likely to reveal true biological signals.\nL198: The present study was the first study to directly compare the performance of Olink and SomaScan proteins for risk prediction of major diseases. Although proteomic prediction models alone did not outperform the performance of the model with conventional risk factors for IHD in analyses of a set of proteins captured by both platforms, the addition of proteins to conventional risk factors improved risk prediction of IHD.\nL199: Adding Olink and SomaScan proteins to conventional risk factors showed comparable performance with similar improvement in C-statistics and positive NRIs. Moreover, the use of all overlapping proteins from both platforms together did not significantly improve the model performance, suggesting that using a set of core proteins from one platform alone may be suitable for risk prediction, at least for IHD.\nL200: Nevertheless, the present analyses focused only on the 1694 overlapping proteins, but there are more proteins measured by the Olink and SomaScan assays that are platform-sepcific, which may further improve risk prediction^{cite125†33 ,cite126†34 }.\nL201: Consistent with previous studies^{cite105†15 }, our Boruta feature selection analyses demonstrated that protein abundance and data quality (e.g. % below LOD and QC warning) were the two most important factors that accounted for the concordance between measurements, as well as differences in downstream genetic and observational analyses between Olink and SomaScan platforms.\nL202: This was further confirmed by our analyses showing that proteins with lower observational correlations, proteins of lower abundance, and proteins with more samples below LOD or more samples with QC warnings in Olink were more likely to yield platform-specific or distinct findings between platforms.\nL203: In particular, while most (>85%) proteins that were significantly associated with BMI or IHD in both platforms showed directionally consistent effects with a high (r > 0.7) correlation of effect sizes, some BMI-associated proteins showed discordant directions of effects between the two platforms. The latter proteins typically had low correlations (median Spearman’s rho = 0.04), possibly reflecting poor data quality.\nL204: Conversely, protein characteristics based on annotations from UniProt Knowledgebase and Gene Ontology (GO) contributed little to the differences between the platforms, at least for the 1694 proteins with one-to-one matched single reagents.\nL205: Nevertheless, discordant findings between platforms might have been influenced by reagents binding to different proteoforms of the same protein, leading to epitope effects and differences in pQTLs^{cite119†9 ,cite83†17 }. Indeed, our additional analyses showed that compared to SomaScan cis-pQTLs that were not related to PAVs, SomaScan PAV cis-pQTLs were more likely to not colocalise with the Olink cis-pQTLs identified for the same protein, suggesting potential epitope effects (Supplementary Fig. cite88†13 ).\nL206: Further analyses of 474 proteins matched to more than one SomaScan or Olink reagent could also explore possible epitope effects. For example, not all SOMAmers targeting TNC showed evidence for colocalisation of cis-pQTLs with the corresponding Olink reagent (Supplementary Figs. cite88†41 , cite88†42 ; Supplementary Data cite95†4 ).\nL207: This could be a result of those reagents targeting different structures of TNC, a protein known to have different isoforms, while several cis-pQTLs identified for TNC in SomaScan were indeed in LD with PAVs (Supplementary Data cite94†3 , cite95†4 )^{cite127†35 ,cite128†36 }. However, in the absence of any data on proteoforms for either platform, it was not possible to reliably assess this hypothesis.\nL208: Finally, we found that proteins with PAV cis-pQTLs were more likely to yield BMI associations shared by both platforms compared to SomaScan-specific associations, (Supplementary Fig. cite88†21 ). This could be because PAV cis-pQTLs, despite potential epitope effects, might also have more functional impact on phenotypes, which could be reliably detected by both platforms. More research is needed to further explore the influence of PAVs on pQTLs and phenotypic associations.\nL209: The present study is the largest study to date that directly compared the utility of antibody and aptamer-based proteomic platforms in identical blood samples. We assessed the agreement between the platforms using both observational and genetic analyses, and compared analytical performance for their associations with traits and IHD, in addition to utility for risk prediction of IHD. However, the present study also had several limitations.\nL210: First, we did not compare coefficients of variation (CV) as a measure of accuracy, as these are strongly influenced by the data distribution. Moreover, we were unable to adopt the CV ratio (i.e. CV in repeated samples divided by CV in unrelated samples) as proposed by Eldjarn et al. to compare accuracy between the platforms^{cite83†17 }, due to the small number of duplicates of proteins/samples measured in the study.\n"
